Superficial facial laceration from fall against furniture edge to chin
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Superficial mental laceration, Furniture strike mechanism, Intact dental occlusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-031
Reason for Visit: Acute, bleeding facial laceration requiring urgent assessment for underlying mandibular fracture and immediate primary closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a blunt facial strike resulting in an open bleeding wound. Urgent bedside assessment was mandated to rule out dental trauma, clear the mandible of fracture, and perform primary wound closure within the golden period to prevent facial scarring and infection before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I tripped and hit my chin on the counter edge, and it split open and won't stop bleeding.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of a simple mechanical trip, striking the chin against a blunt counter edge 1 hour ago. This resulted in a small linear and slightly jagged skin break strictly confined to the dermal layers of the mental region. There was minor localized bleeding that was easily and rapidly controlled with simple direct pressure. The patient complains of mild localized stinging pain directly over the point of impact. There is a complete absence of any altered mental status, amnesia, or loss of consciousness surrounding the event.
Functional Status: Fully independent; holding a gauze pad to the chin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occasional mild unsteadiness due to reading glasses; advised to ensure clear walking paths.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, cooperative with the examination.
Physical Exam: Maxillofacial examination reveals a superficial mental laceration without high tension. Wound edges are easily apposed manually. There is a total absence of an underlying palpable step-off deformity or bony crepitus of the mandible. Normal dental alignment is observed without newly acquired malocclusion or loose teeth, confirming an intact dental occlusion. Intact temporomandibular joint function is present, allowing full, pain-free maximal mouth opening. Neurological status is intact (GCS 15).
Environmental Observations: Kitchen counter edge identified as the strike zone; clear of sharp protruding nails.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial facial laceration to chin - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Mandibular fracture - Ruled out due to: Intact dental occlusion, normal maximal mouth opening, and absence of bony step-off or crepitus.

Traumatic brain injury / Concussion - Ruled out due to: GCS 15, no loss of consciousness, and negative history of vomiting or severe headache.

Dental avulsion/subluxation - Ruled out due to: Visually intact dentition with no mobility of the mandibular incisors upon palpation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Blunt trauma to the chin carries a high index of suspicion for hidden mandibular or condylar fractures due to force transmission. Rigorous physical examination confidently excluded underlying skeletal or dental trauma, isolating the pathology to a superficial dermal break. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to rule out these structural injuries and perform immediate, cosmetically sensitive wound closure with tissue adhesive, preventing secondary infection and minimizing permanent facial scarring, which would be compromised by a delayed daytime presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wound irrigated gently with sterile saline. Hemostasis secured. Wound closed with tissue adhesive (cyanoacrylate) and reinforced with sterile skin closure strips. Tetanus prophylaxis updated on site.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the chin completely dry for 48 hours and avoid excessive jaw movement like chewing hard foods. This prevents mechanical stress from cracking the tissue glue, ensuring the wound heals rapidly as a thin, neat line rather than a thick, widened scar.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days for a wound check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop worsening headaches, repetitive vomiting, confusion, or if you suddenly notice your teeth do not bite together correctly.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Minor hairline fractures of the jaw can occasionally present with delayed pain; monitor bite alignment closely.
Clinical Course: Mild stinging will resolve within 24 hours. The tissue glue will naturally flake off in 5 to 10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the wound becomes hot, excessively red, starts discharging thick yellow pus, or if you have difficulty swallowing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Tissue adhesive acts as a waterproof seal, holding the edges together precisely for optimal cosmetic healing without the need for painful facial injections and sutures.
Actionable Steps: Eat soft foods for the next two days. Do not scrub the face when washing. Sleep on your back to avoid rubbing the chin against the pillow.

